<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/intermediate/research-methods">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Psychology's hardest problem is that its subject — a person — knows it is being studied, has
    moods, and differs from every other person. Physics gets to assume all electrons are alike;
    psychology's participants are not, and many will change their behaviour the moment they
    suspect what you want. The discipline's answer is <strong>method</strong>: a set of
    procedures for telling real effects from expectations, coincidences and wishful thinking.
    Master the toolkit below and you gain something rare — the ability to read a headline claim
    ("study shows…") and know within a minute whether it deserves your trust.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five-step skeleton of an experiment" widget="equationStep" config={methodCfg} />

  <h2>The designs: six ways to collect evidence</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Experiments</strong> are the only design that can establish cause, because the
    researcher manipulates an independent variable (IV) and measures a dependent variable (DV)
    while holding everything else still. Laboratory experiments give maximum control at the cost
    of artificiality; field experiments (in schools, streets, hospitals) buy realism with noise;
    <strong>natural/quasi-experiments</strong> exploit events the researcher could not arrange —
    comparing children before and after a natural disaster, or identical twins reared apart —
    and sacrifice random assignment, so cause and effect get murkier.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other five designs trade causal certainty for access to things experiments cannot
    ethically or practically touch:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Self-reports</strong> (questionnaires, interviews) reach beliefs, feelings and
    histories that cannot be observed directly — but depend on honest, accurate
    introspection. Social desirability bias is the constant threat: people under-report what
    embarrasses them. Closed questions give easy-to-analyse numbers; open questions give rich
    material that is harder to compare.</li>
    <li><strong>Case studies</strong> follow one person or a few in extraordinary depth — HM,
    whose surgery removed his ability to form new memories and rewrote neuroscience's map of
    memory; Genie, the girl raised in isolation. Irreplaceable for rare conditions, but
    generalising from n = 1 is perilous.</li>
    <li><strong>Observations</strong> record behaviour as it happens, in naturalistic settings
    (real world, no interference) or controlled ones (a playroom wired with cameras). Covert
    watching avoids demand characteristics — people performing for the researcher — but raises
    privacy questions; participant observation (the researcher joins in) gains trust at the risk
    of losing objectivity.</li>
    <li><strong>Correlations</strong> measure how two variables co-vary in existing data —
    scatterplots and a coefficient from −1 to +1. Massive datasets, no manipulation, and the
    standing invitation to confuse association with cause.</li>
    <li><strong>Longitudinal studies</strong> track the same people for years or decades — the
    only way to watch development unfold within persons (the 1946 British birth cohort has been
    followed for nearly 80 years). Powerful against "which came first", but expensive, slow, and
    bleeding participants at every stage (attrition) — and the ones who stay may differ
    systematically from the ones who leave.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>From question to hypothesis</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>aim</strong> states what the study is investigating ("to test whether sleep loss
    impairs memory"). A <strong>hypothesis</strong> is a precise, testable prediction about
    variables — and it must be <em>falsifiable</em>: state what result would prove it wrong
    (Popper's demand; an idea that no observation could refute is not scientific). A
    <strong>directional hypothesis</strong> commits to a direction ("sleep-deprived participants
    will recall <em>fewer</em> words"), used when prior research points that way; a
    <strong>non-directional</strong> one predicts "a difference" without saying which way.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Operationalisation</strong> is where real research gets hard: turning ideas into
    measurable things. "Stress" becomes salivary cortisol, heart rate, or a score on a validated
    questionnaire — pick one, define it, and accept that you have thereby chosen one
    interpretation of stress. "Memory" becomes "number of words recalled from a 20-word list
    after 10 minutes". Vague measures produce vague science; precise measures produce precise
    (and contestable) science. Operational definitions are always debatable — which is why
    good papers defend theirs explicitly.
  </p>

  <h2>Control: the war on confounds</h2>
  <p>
    Any variable other than the IV that systematically affects the DV is a
    <strong>confounding variable</strong>, and it is the researcher's enemy. Sources are legion:
    participant variables (mood, caffeine, sleep), situational variables (noisy room, time of
    day), <strong>demand characteristics</strong> (participants guessing the hypothesis and
    playing along), and <strong>investigator effects</strong> (the experimenter's tone, gender or
    expectations leaking into results — Rosenthal and Fode's 1963 study found students told
    their rats were "bright" recorded better maze times than students told their rats were
    "dull"; the rats were randomly assigned and identical). The standard arsenal:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Random assignment</strong> to conditions — the great equaliser, distributing
    every unmeasured difference (motivation, luck, sleep) evenly between groups.</li>
    <li><strong>Control groups</strong> — the comparison that receives nothing, so the treatment
    group has something honest to be measured against.</li>
    <li><strong>Counterbalancing</strong> — half the participants get condition A then B, half B
    then A, cancelling order effects like practice and fatigue.</li>
    <li><strong>Standardisation</strong> — identical instructions, timing and environment for
    everyone, so procedure differences cannot masquerade as treatment effects.</li>
    <li><strong>Blinding</strong> — single-blind (participants don't know their condition) and
    double-blind (experimenters don't either), because expectation alone produces real measured
    effects.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Only the randomised experiment fully breaks the correlation traps. <strong>Reverse
    causation</strong> (does anxiety cause insomnia or the reverse?), <strong>third
    variables</strong> (ice-cream sales and drownings rise together because of summer, not
    dessert), and <strong>selection effects</strong> (hospital patients are sicker than the
    public — that is why they are there) all fall to the same move: assign the "cause"
    randomly, and any pre-existing difference loses its ability to impersonate the treatment.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a large sample fixes a biased one. A survey of 100,000
  readers of one partisan website is precisely measured nonsense. Randomness, not size, buys
  generalisability — a well-drawn sample of 1,000 can represent a nation better than a badly
  drawn one of a million.</p>

  <h2>Sampling: who gets studied</h2>
  <p>
    You cannot test everyone, so you draw a <strong>sample</strong> from the target population —
    and every sampling method encodes a bias risk. <strong>Random sampling</strong> (everyone has
    an equal chance, via a list and a randomiser) is ideal but needs the full population list,
    which rarely exists. <strong>Stratified</strong> sampling preserves subgroups in proportion —
    if 20% of the school is sixth-form, 20% of the sample is. <strong>Systematic</strong> sampling
    takes every nth name. <strong>Opportunity sampling</strong> (whoever is available) and
    <strong>volunteer/self-selected sampling</strong> (responding to adverts) are cheap and
    heavily used — and both over-represent the available, the cooperative and the motivated,
    which is exactly how psychology ended up studying so many undergraduates for course credit.
  </p>

  <h2>Data and analysis: counting what you found</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Types of data.</strong> Quantitative data are numbers — comparable, compact,
    statistically powerful, but lossy ("8/10 on a mood scale" hides <em>which</em> 10).
    Qualitative data are words — interviews, diaries, observation transcripts — rich in meaning
    and context, but harder to compare and vulnerable to researcher interpretation. Data also
    divide by measurement level: <strong>nominal</strong> (categories: yes/no, blood type),
    <strong>ordinal</strong> (ranked: finish position, Likert scales — order yes, equal gaps
    unknown), and <strong>interval</strong> (true numbers with equal gaps: seconds, degrees
    Celsius). The level decides the arithmetic you are allowed to do.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Descriptive statistics</strong> summarise: the mean (average — sensitive to extreme
    scores), median (middle score — robust to outliers), mode (most frequent — the only option
    for nominal data), plus the spread: range for a rough measure, standard deviation for how
    tightly scores cluster around the mean. Two groups can share a mean and differ completely in
    variability, which is why reporting both is mandatory in honest work.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Inferential statistics</strong> then ask: could this pattern be a fluke of sampling?
    A significance test yields a <strong>p-value</strong> — the probability of seeing a result at
    least this extreme if there were really no effect. By convention p under 0.05 counts as
    "statistically significant", but read it correctly: it is evidence against the null
    hypothesis, <em>not</em> proof of yours, and it says nothing about how big the effect is.
    For that, report an <strong>effect size</strong> (such as Cohen's d) and, for correlations,
    the coefficient itself. A significant-but-tiny effect in a huge sample can be real and
    trivial at the same time.
  </p>

  <h2>Ethics: the non-negotiables</h2>
  <p>
    Psychological research runs on people's trust, and the professional codes (the BPS Code of
    Ethics in the UK, the APA's in the US) set the floor: <strong>informed consent</strong>
    (participants know they are in a study and roughly what it involves), <strong>protection
    from harm</strong> (no lasting physical or psychological damage — and no more distress than
    necessary), <strong>right to withdraw</strong> at any time without penalty (including having
    their data removed later), <strong>confidentiality</strong> (no identifiable data without
    permission), <strong>deception</strong> only when unavoidable and trivial, and a full
    <strong>debrief</strong> revealing anything withheld. Studies are reviewed in advance by
    ethics committees, which weigh scientific value against participant burden.
  </p>
  <p>
    History explains the strictness. Milgram's obedience experiments (1961–63) deceived
    participants into believing they were delivering dangerous electric shocks; many left
    shaking, and the studies remain the boundary marker the codes were written against. The
    Tuskegee syphilis study — where treatment was withheld from Black men for 40 years — is the
    darker ancestor of modern consent rules. The hard cases are real: can you study obedience at
    all without deception? Can a natural experiment (a disaster) ever be ethically arranged?
    The codes do not answer these; they force the researcher to justify the trade-off openly.
  </p>

  <h2>Validity, reliability, replicability</h2>
  <p>
    A study is <strong>valid</strong> if it measures what it claims to. <strong>Internal
    validity</strong>: did the IV really cause the DV change, or did a confound? (Blinding,
    control groups and standardisation serve this.) <strong>External validity</strong>: do the
    findings travel — to other people (population validity), other settings (ecological
    validity), other times? A laboratory memory task with 20 psychology students may be
    internally airtight and ecologically irrelevant. <strong>Construct validity</strong> asks
    whether the operationalisation really captures the concept: is a 20-question quiz
    "intelligence", or just reading speed plus test-taking practice?
  </p>
  <p>
    A measure is <strong>reliable</strong> if it gives consistent results: <strong>test–retest
    reliability</strong> (same person, similar score two weeks later), <strong>inter-observer
    reliability</strong> (two independent observers of the same behaviour record the same thing
    — the fix for subjective coding is training, agreed behavioural categories and correlation
    of their records). Note the relationship: a measure can be reliable without being valid —
    a bathroom scale that always reads 3 kg heavy is perfectly reliable and completely invalid.
    Validity without reliability is impossible; inconsistent measurement destroys any claim.
    <strong>Replicability</strong> is reliability's scientific-level cousin: can an independent
    team, following your published method, get your result again? A finding that only one lab
    can produce is a rumour.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "psychology isn't a real science because people are
  unpredictable." Unpredictable individuals produce predictable aggregates — insurance works on
  the same principle — and psychology's subject matter being difficult is an argument for more
  method, not less. The science question is decided by the procedures, and psychology's
  procedures (measurement, control, falsification, replication) are the same ones chemistry
  uses, adapted to participants who can refuse.</p>

  <h2>Psychometric tests: measuring the measurers</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>psychometric test</strong> is a standardised instrument for measuring a
    psychological trait — IQ batteries, the Big Five personality inventory, depression scales
    like the PHQ-9, the SAT. What separates a real one from a magazine quiz is the validation
    file: norms from large standardisation samples (so your score has a meaning — "87th
    percentile among people like you"), documented <strong>reliability</strong> (internal
    consistency measured with Cronbach's alpha, typically demanded above 0.7; test–retest
    stability), and <strong>validity evidence</strong> — does the score predict the things it
    claims to predict (concurrent validity against established measures; predictive validity
    against later outcomes)? Psychometrics is also where psychology's social responsibilities
    are sharpest: tests gate education, employment and diagnosis, so cultural bias in items,
    unfair norms, and reifying constructs (treating "IQ" as a thing rather than a score) are
    not academic quibbles but consequential errors. The tools exist to be audited — good tests
    publish their manuals, norms and error estimates; bad ones publish vibes.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The replication crisis — and the repair.</strong> Between 2011 and 2015 psychology
    audited itself, and the audit stung: the Open Science Collaboration (2015) reran 100 famous
    studies and could reproduce the original "significant" result in only about 36% of them.
    The causes were structural, not fraudulent (mostly): small samples gave noisy results;
    journals published surprising positives, so researchers hunted them; flexible analysis
    ("p-hacking", trying tests until one passed) turned noise into publishable "findings". The
    repair changed daily practice: <strong>pre-registration</strong> (announce hypotheses and
    analysis before seeing data), much larger samples with power calculations, open data and
    code, <strong>registered reports</strong> (journals accept papers before results exist),
    and multi-lab collaborations (Many Labs, the 2018 Moral Machine-style replications) that
    test effects dozens of times at once. Psychology is now, by a wide margin, the most
    self-scrutinising science of the lot — which is what doing science honestly looks like.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>WEIRD samples.</strong> Henrich, Heine and Norenzayan's 2010 bombshell documented
    that the typical psychology participant was Western, educated, industrialised, rich and
    democratic — and that on some measures (visual illusions, fairness in economic games,
    conformity) WEIRD populations are statistical
    outliers of humanity (the Müller-Lyer illusion is a famous example); cross-cultural replication is
    now a respected field in its own right, and "student sample, WEIRD caveat" is a standard
    limitation paragraph in published papers.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The p-value's tempestuous century.</strong> Ronald Fisher set 0.05 as a rough
    publishing bar in the 1920s; a century of treating it as a magic threshold produced the
    crisis above. Modern best practice reports exact p-values, effect sizes and confidence
    intervals, and increasingly favours Bayesian and estimation approaches that ask "how big is
    the effect, how certain are we" rather than "did we cross the line". The deeper lesson of
    research methods is the one that survives every methodological fashion: knowledge is what
    remains after the checks have had their shot.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Popper, The Logic of Scientific Discovery; Open Science Collaboration, "Estimating the reproducibility of psychological science", Science (2015); Henrich, Heine & Norenzayan, "The weirdest people in the world?", BBS (2010); BPS Code of Human Research Ethics; American Psychological Association ethics materials.</p>
